{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When a man or a woman shall clearly utter a vow, a nazirite vow.</b> This passage of naziriteship was juxtaposed to the passage of the sotah to teach that whoever sees a sotah in her corruption should become a nazir, abstaining from wine — so did our Rabbis of blessed memory expound, and thus it says (Hosea 4:11): ״זנות יין ותירוש״ — harlotry, wine, and new wine — ״ותירוש יקח לב״ — and new wine takes away the heart." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): the nazirite was warned against wine because everyone drawn after it is drawn after desire, and it leads to many sins and stumbling-blocks. And he was commanded regarding the growth of hair because it breeds anxiety, and his heart will be humbled in the service of HaShem when he is disheveled in his hair, and he will be cautious about the excesses that dishevel him. For hair is the surplus of nature in man, from the vapors generated in the body. And this is the sense of יפליא — he does a wondrous thing, for most of the world go after their desires and this one separates from desires. And this is the sense of נזיר, from the language of (Leviticus 22:2): ״ונזרו מקדשי בני ישראל״ — that is, the language of separation." "<b>Or</b> it may be that נזיר is from the language of נזר הקדש, the crown of holiness, for נזר אלהיו על ראשו — the crown of his Elohim is upon his head — and this is the concept of a diadem and a royal crown. He is called by this name because he is a king who rules over his desires, unlike the rest of the people of the world, who are servants to desire." "<b>To consecrate himself to YHWH.</b> For his intent in naziriteship is for YHWH, since wine impairs reason (sekhel), and it is impossible for a person's service to be complete."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:29:54.550546807Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}